13. DP on strings — table setup pattern
    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];   // +1 for empty-string base case
    // dp[i][j] usually means: answer using first i chars of a, first j chars of b
    // base case: dp[0][j] = ... , dp[i][0] = ...
    // transition compares a.charAt(i-1) with b.charAt(j-1)

14. Common gotchas
    - substring(start, end) → end is EXCLUSIVE, easy to get off-by-one
    - Never compare strings with == — always .equals()
    - s.charAt(i) throws error if i >= s.length() — check bounds first
    - Empty string "" vs null are different — check both when unsure
    - int[26] only works for lowercase a-z; use int[128] or HashMap otherwise
    - Modifying a String doesn't change the original (immutability) — must reassign or use StringBuilder
    - Integer.parseInt(s) throws exception on invalid input — wrap in try/catch if unsure of input
